WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
The boosting fibroblast growth factor 13 from astrocytes slows aging throughout the bodies of mice. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 229. Restricted feeding and mutation load, and Alzheimer variant in human neurons, and high glucose in tumor-fighting T cells come after that mouse work. The finding appears in aging cell, and astrocytes are the glial cells that support neuronal function in the brain. After targeted elevation of the factor, aging markers were scored in tissues outside

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.960
the brain. Changes reach the rest of the body, even though only those brain support cells

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.709
were altered. The intervention acts on a signaling molecule released by support cells rather

00:00:40.709 --> 00:00:46.409
than on peripheral organs themselves. Earlier work had tied astrocyte-derived factors to

00:00:46.409 --> 00:00:52.229
local brain maintenance, and this study extends that reach beyond the skull. Signals that start

00:00:52.229 --> 00:00:57.469
in the central nervous system shifted organism-wide aging trajectories, and similar signals

00:00:57.469 --> 00:01:00.229
mammals can now be tested for modulation in other mammals.

00:01:01.363 --> 00:01:06.383
Restricted feeding lowered somatic mutation accumulation across tissues in work published

00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:06.923
in cell.

00:01:07.623 --> 00:01:11.883
Mutation rates were measured in animals on caloric restriction and set against parallel

00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:13.543
cohorts tracked for lifespan.

00:01:14.323 --> 00:01:17.243
The drop in mutations was consistent from tissue to tissue.

00:01:17.763 --> 00:01:22.803
That drop still did not account for the lifespan extension recorded under the same feeding conditions.

00:01:22.803 --> 00:01:27.403
medicines. The design separates mutation load from other processes that operate during

00:01:27.403 --> 00:01:33.083
caloric restriction. Improved proteostasis and reduced inflammation are named as processes

00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:35.683
that may contribute more to the life span effect.

00:01:36.643 --> 00:01:43.103
CRISPR edited human neuronal cultures flagged a C-risk allele in the gene PTK2B as the most

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:49.703
functionally relevant Alzheimer linked variant in a BioRXIV report. Cells carrying two copies

00:01:49.703 --> 00:01:56.643
of that sea allele showed more than 80% reduction in total PYK2 protein, while the share of phosphorylated

00:01:56.643 --> 00:02:05.203
PYK2 rose. The risk genotype raised tau phosphorylation at 3-onine 181, 3-onine 217,

00:02:05.803 --> 00:02:14.603
3-onine 231, serine 202 through 3-onine 205, and serine 396 through serine 404.

00:02:14.603 --> 00:02:22.043
Levels of soluble amyloid precursor protein beta, or SAPP beta, and of amyloid beta also

00:02:22.043 --> 00:02:22.623
increased.

00:02:23.263 --> 00:02:28.383
Transcriptomic profiling found widespread changes in genes tied to extracellular matrix

00:02:28.383 --> 00:02:31.083
organization and neurotransmitter secretion.

00:02:31.723 --> 00:02:36.423
Multi-electrode array recordings showed reduced network recruitment and altered burst patterns

00:02:36.423 --> 00:02:37.823
in those neuronal cultures.

00:02:39.004 --> 00:02:44.244
CD8 positive T cells gained a factor function against cancer when blood glucose was high.

00:02:44.244 --> 00:02:46.564
according to the Journal of Immunology.

00:02:46.924 --> 00:02:52.664
The work used mouse tumor models and isolated human T cells placed in high glucose conditions.

00:02:53.184 --> 00:02:58.584
Elevated glucose enhanced T cell metabolic activity and effector responses in tumor settings.

00:02:59.024 --> 00:03:02.904
The benefit was traced to changes in T cell signaling and energy use.

00:03:03.364 --> 00:03:06.084
The paper supplies a mechanistic account of that effect.

00:03:06.644 --> 00:03:11.304
Higher glucose availability supported immune attack on cancer cells in these settings rather

00:03:11.304 --> 00:03:12.244
than impairing it.

00:03:12.244 --> 00:03:18.121
Most people assume a living cell cannot run its core chemistry unless every protein is built

00:03:18.121 --> 00:03:19.921
from all 20 amino acids.

00:03:20.841 --> 00:03:25.481
Proteins that carry out core reactions today descend from ancestors that worked when fewer

00:03:25.481 --> 00:03:27.161
amino acid types existed.

00:03:27.701 --> 00:03:33.941
A reconstructed ancestral enzyme still generated ATP after a modern required amino acid was swapped

00:03:33.941 --> 00:03:37.721
for a simpler substitute in a report carried by Fizz.org.

00:03:37.721 --> 00:03:43.641
That enzyme sits in cellular energy metabolism, so continued function under reduced chemical

00:03:43.641 --> 00:03:48.381
complexity is a direct test of how early life could have kept essential processes running.

00:03:48.901 --> 00:03:54.021
The same pathway operates in human muscle, brain, and every other tissue that depends on

00:03:54.021 --> 00:03:55.661
ATP from moment to moment.

00:03:56.201 --> 00:04:00.941
Researchers rebuilt the ancestral sequence and then tested activity after the substitution

00:04:00.941 --> 00:04:02.061
at the critical site.

00:04:02.061 --> 00:04:06.921
Catalytic activity persisted, even though the chain was paired back at that position.

00:04:07.601 --> 00:04:12.481
ATP output was measured under controlled laboratory conditions, and the simplified enzyme

00:04:12.481 --> 00:04:14.201
retained measurable activity.

00:04:14.761 --> 00:04:19.741
ATP production continued without the amino acid that the modern version uses for its usual

00:04:19.741 --> 00:04:20.221
reaction.

00:04:20.841 --> 00:04:25.581
Some sites appear able to accept simpler building blocks that were available earlier in evolutionary

00:04:25.581 --> 00:04:26.301
history.

00:04:26.301 --> 00:04:32.341
Extrachemical complexity in modern sequences may serve regulation or stability rather than

00:04:32.341 --> 00:04:33.761
the basic reaction itself.

00:04:34.261 --> 00:04:38.821
Experiments that place these paired-down enzymes back into living cells would test whether

00:04:38.821 --> 00:04:42.661
test tube tolerance holds when crowding and competing reactions are present.

00:04:43.261 --> 00:04:48.001
If the simplified forms still work, they could inform minimal protein design for synthetic

00:04:48.001 --> 00:04:53.701
biology, where fewer required amino acids can lower production costs or improve stability.

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:59.401
The study therefore supplies a window into early biology and a benchmark for how far a sequence

00:04:59.401 --> 00:05:02.041
can be trimmed without losing its central activity.

00:05:02.793 --> 00:05:07.853
Teams at the Institute of Science Tokyo, the University of Zagreb Faculty of Science,

00:05:08.273 --> 00:05:14.933
and ETH Zurich report that evolutionary changes in isolusal TRNA synthetase help bacteria

00:05:14.933 --> 00:05:17.073
fine-tune the speed of protein production.

00:05:17.693 --> 00:05:21.473
The enzyme charges TRNA with isolucine during translation.

00:05:21.473 --> 00:05:26.593
Allterations and enzyme structure appear to adjust the rate at which proteins are assembled.

00:05:27.413 --> 00:05:31.973
Modern bacterial versions were compared with inferred ancestral forms to identify those

00:05:31.973 --> 00:05:37.833
functional shifts. The changes allow bacteria to balance accuracy and speed in protein synthesis

00:05:37.833 --> 00:05:43.293
under varying environmental conditions. Core translation machinery can evolve in ways that

00:05:43.293 --> 00:05:49.268
optimize cellular growth rates. Scientists at St. Jude Children's Research Hospital identified

00:05:49.268 --> 00:05:56.368
how one factor involved in messenger RNA decoding can itself be altered, with the work appearing in nature cell biology.

00:05:56.888 --> 00:06:00.768
The factor participates in translation fidelity during protein synthesis.

00:06:01.448 --> 00:06:06.828
Modification of that factor changes how accurately messenger RNA is read into protein sequences.

00:06:07.588 --> 00:06:14.048
Errors introduced at this step can promote oncogenic signaling, particularly in cells that carry K-RAS mutations.

00:06:14.048 --> 00:06:19.528
The results point to potential cancer origins and to new targets for K-RAS-related treatment

00:06:19.528 --> 00:06:20.188
resistance.

00:06:20.888 --> 00:06:25.508
Identification of regulatory inputs on the factor provides fresh entry points for studying

00:06:25.508 --> 00:06:27.468
translation linked cancer mechanisms.

00:06:27.948 --> 00:06:34.102
A collaborative team led by Yasuhiro Kobori at Kobe University, with colleagues at Toho

00:06:34.102 --> 00:06:40.602
University, Saitama University, Nagoya Institute of Technology, and the University of Freiburg,

00:06:40.602 --> 00:06:45.122
work, identified a molecular mechanism that could enhance magnetic compass responses

00:06:45.122 --> 00:06:47.842
in cryptochrome, according to Fizz.org.

00:06:48.322 --> 00:06:52.722
The work centers on a blue light-sensitive protein implicated in magnetoreception.

00:06:53.342 --> 00:06:58.082
Specific twists at the molecular level alter how the protein responds to magnetic fields.

00:06:58.582 --> 00:07:03.342
Those twists change the protein's interaction with radical pairs formed upon light absorption.

00:07:04.022 --> 00:07:09.082
The study examined how such structural changes influence sensitivity to weak magnetic fields.

00:07:09.082 --> 00:07:14.482
Cryptochrome proteins are found in the eyes of migratory birds and other animals that navigate

00:07:14.482 --> 00:07:16.362
using the Earth's magnetic field.

00:07:17.273 --> 00:07:22.473
Polyethylene, polyethylene, tariffelate, and polypropylene particles were aged in the

00:07:22.473 --> 00:07:27.293
laboratory so their later binding to contaminants could be measured under controlled conditions.

00:07:28.093 --> 00:07:31.053
Microplastics measure less than 5mm in length.

00:07:31.733 --> 00:07:35.653
Aging altered surface properties of the three polymer types in distinct ways.

00:07:35.653 --> 00:07:41.093
Those surface changes influenced how readily the particles bound other chemical contaminants

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:42.673
in surrounding water or soil.

00:07:43.313 --> 00:07:48.193
The protocol mimics environmental exposure over time without waiting for natural weathering.

00:07:48.613 --> 00:07:53.773
The approach allows tests of microplastic behavior that would otherwise require long environmental

00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:54.253
weights.

00:07:55.236 --> 00:08:00.136
Researchers at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem and partner institutions built an artificial

00:08:00.136 --> 00:08:03.916
intelligence tool that helps interpret genetic data from soil samples.

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:10.376
A handful of soil holds a crowded mix of bacteria, fungi, and other organisms whose DNA is

00:08:10.376 --> 00:08:11.516
difficult to identify.

00:08:12.116 --> 00:08:17.796
The tool analyzes complex genetic sequences and processes metagenomic data to assign likely

00:08:17.796 --> 00:08:21.236
identities to previously unclassified bacterial genomes.

00:08:21.796 --> 00:08:26.336
The method improves resolution of microbial communities that standard database searches

00:08:26.336 --> 00:08:27.356
leave unresolved.

00:08:27.876 --> 00:08:31.916
Soil microbes play central roles in nutrient cycling and plant health.

00:08:31.916 --> 00:08:37.196
Better identification tools matter for ecology and agriculture research that depends on

00:08:37.196 --> 00:08:38.996
knowing which organisms are present.

00:08:40.029 --> 00:08:45.789
You Kinjo and Professor Ryugotaro, working with Toyohashi University of Technology, the

00:08:45.789 --> 00:08:51.129
Institute for Molecular Science and the Institute of Science Tokyo, combined x-ray absorption

00:08:51.129 --> 00:08:56.189
spectroscopy with data analysis to isolate faint water signals at material surfaces.

00:08:56.829 --> 00:09:01.429
The approach separates weak signals of interfacial water from those of surrounding water.

00:09:01.429 --> 00:09:07.589
Interfacial water layers influence chemical reactions and material properties at solid liquid boundaries.

00:09:07.929 --> 00:09:13.169
The separation reduces interference from bulk water signals that normally overwhelm the measurement.

00:09:13.749 --> 00:09:19.269
The method was applied to model systems to confirm it isolates the desired surface-specific information.

00:09:19.789 --> 00:09:25.569
That capability opens routes to study hydration structures relevant to catalysis and biomaterials.

00:09:25.569 --> 00:09:31.103
The Ching Hai Tibet Plateau is the setting for a reconstruction of how terrestrial animals

00:09:31.103 --> 00:09:34.663
assembled across three distinct regions over millions of years.

00:09:35.283 --> 00:09:39.563
Extreme elevation and climate created unique selective pressures on that fauna.

00:09:40.243 --> 00:09:45.003
Fossil, genetic, and geographic data were integrated to trace diversification patterns.

00:09:45.663 --> 00:09:49.943
The analysis revealed different assembly histories for the three distinct regions examined.

00:09:49.943 --> 00:09:55.303
Tectonic uplift and climatic shifts shape the biodiversity now found on the plateau.

00:09:55.663 --> 00:10:00.083
The work offers a picture of how the region's distinctive animals developed under those

00:10:00.083 --> 00:10:00.503
pressures.

00:10:01.567 --> 00:10:06.747
Before we go, placing these simplified ancestral enzymes back into living cells is the next

00:10:06.747 --> 00:10:11.407
test of whether test tube ATP activity survives crowding and competing reactions.

